      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To solve for \\(\\tan V\\) in the right triangle \\(VWX\\) where \\(VX = \\sqrt{13}\\) and \\(VW = 3\\), we can follow these steps:\n\n1. **Identify the right angle**: Since \\(VWX\\) is a right triangle, the angle \\(V\\) is the right angle. Therefore, \\(\\tan V = \\tan 90^\\circ\\).\n\n2. **Recall the definition of \\(\\tan\\) in a right triangle**: The tangent of an angle in a right triangle is the ratio of the length of the opposite side to the length of the adjacent side. For angle \\(V\\) (which is the right angle), the opposite side is the leg \\(WX\\) and the adjacent side is the leg \\(VW\\).\n\n3. **Determine the lengths of the sides**: We need to find the length of \\(WX\\). Using the Pythagorean theorem in triangle \\(VWX\\), we have:\n   \\[\n   VX^2 = VW^2 + WX^2\n   \\]\n   Substituting the given values:\n   \\[\n   (\\sqrt{13})^2 = 3^2 + WX^2\n   \\]\n   Simplifying both sides:\n   \\[\n   13 = 9 + WX^2\n   \\]\n   Solving for \\(WX^2\\):\n   \\[\n   WX^2 = 13 - 9 = 4\n   \\]\n   Taking the square root of both sides:\n   \\[\n   WX = 2\n   \\]\n\n4. **Calculate \\(\\tan V\\)**: Now that we know the lengths of the opposite and adjacent sides are \\(WX = 2\\) and \\(VW = 3\\) respectively, we can find \\(\\tan V\\):\n   \\[\n   \\tan V = \\frac{WX}{VW} = \\frac{2}{3}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\(\\tan V\\) is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{2}{3}}\\).",
